          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em formal group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal space
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %em first order infinitesimal
            spaces, formal groups may be of arbitrary infinitesimal order. They sit between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            and finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+groups"} algebraic groups
          %p
            Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+spaces"} infinitesimal spaces
            are typically modeled as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} formal duals
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras"} algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            algebras.
          %p
            Specifically, fixing a formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate
            chart, then the product operation of a formal group is entirely expressed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in two variables, satisfying conditions. This is called a
            = succeed "," do
              %em formal group law
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Lazard"} Lazard
          %p
            Commutative formal group laws of dimension 1 notably appear in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            (originating in work by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Novikov"} Novikov
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Buchstaber"} Buchstaber
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Quillen
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Adams74"} Adams 74, part II
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
            evaluated on infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+projective+space"} complex projective space
            (i.e. on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(1)
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq \mathbb{C}P^\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism"} complex cobordism
            cohomology theory in this context gives the universal formal group law represented by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lazard+ring"} Lazard ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/height+of+a+formal+group"} height of formal groups
            induces a filtering on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
            called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic filtration
          %p
            More recently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fabien+Morel"} Morel
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Levine"} Marc Levine
            consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+cobordism"} algebraic cobordism
            of smooth schemes in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+field+theory"} class field theory
          %h3#FormalGroupLaw Formal group laws
          #AdicRing.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              An (commutative)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+ring"} adic ring
              is a (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"}> commutative
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+ring"} topological ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and an ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \subset A
              such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
                  on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  is the
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+topology"} adic topology
              %li
                %p the canonical morphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⟶
                        %msub
                          %munder
                            %mi lim
                            %mo ⟵
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        A \longrightarrow \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_n (A/I^n)
                %p
                  to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                  over
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+rings"} quotient rings
                  by powers of the ideal is an
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of adic rings is a ring
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              that is also a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              (hence a function that preserves the filtering
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊃
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ⊃
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⊃
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \supset \cdots \supset A/I^2 \supset A/I
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AdicRing
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdicRing
              and a subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AdicCRing
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdicCRing
              of commutative adic rings.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AdicRing
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdicRing
              (on
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+rings"} Noetherian rings
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+schemes"} formal schemes
            %p
              Similarly, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              any fixed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              are
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  adic
                  = succeed "-algebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
